# The weights of a three-layer ground material, in the one arrangement whose answer is closed-form.
#
# ⚠ Every mask here is a CONSTANT, which is not a shortcut. A constant mask is folded into the
# layer's opacity by `LayerStackGraph.Folds`, so this exercises the same arithmetic a painted mask
# reaches and gives a value a test can predict without a second implementation of the compositor.
#
# Resolved by `over`, top down: snow takes half the texel, moss a quarter of what is left, and rock
# the rest — 0.375, 0.125, 0.5 in R, G and B, summing to one, with an alpha of nothing because there
# is no fourth layer. Every one of those numbers is different from every other, so a permuted pack,
# an unresolved pack and an invented alpha are three different wrong pictures.
#
# ⚠ Snow's `blend: Multiply` is the one line here that is not about that arithmetic. `Blend` mixes by
# the operator after scaling by the amount, so over the black backdrop a coverage build starts from, a
# Multiply layer mixes to black and would weigh NOTHING however much of the texel it covers.
# `LayerStackSplat.Coverage` composites every layer with Copy for exactly that reason; without this
# line the fixture could not tell that decision from its absence.
version: 1
name: Splat Stack
baseWidth: 64
baseHeight: 64
sets:
  - name: Ground
    channels:
      - usage: baseColor
        default: [0, 0, 0, 1]
    layers:
      - id: rock
        name: Rock
        kind: Fill
        values:
          baseColor: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 1]
      - id: moss
        name: Moss
        kind: Fill
        mask:
          source: Constant
          value: 0.25
        values:
          baseColor: [0.2, 0.6, 0.2, 1]
      - id: snow
        name: Snow
        kind: Fill
        blend: Multiply
        mask:
          source: Constant
          value: 0.5
        values:
          baseColor: [0.9, 0.9, 0.95, 1]
